{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg2zpP9ZZjencWVciRSws","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The CSV reader now handles quoted fields that contain commas. `cd N03_csv && node test.js` prints `OK` and exits 0.\n\n**Cause:** `parseLine` used `line.split(\",\")`, so a value like `\"Chennai, South\"` was cut into two cells. That pushed `amount` into the wrong column on rows 240–255, and those 16 rows came out as bad rows.\n\n**Fix (`N03_csv/broken.js` only):** I replaced the split with a small parser that reads one character at a time:\n- Commas inside double quotes are treated as part of the value, and the quotes themselves are removed.\n- A doubled quote (`\"\"`) inside a quoted field becomes a literal `\"`.\n- Line splitting now also accepts Windows line endings (`\\r\\n`), in case the export comes from Windows.\n\n**Result:** the total is 2555295, matching the expected value, with 0 bad rows. Row 245's city now reads `Delhi, North`, so the test's last check passes. I didn't change any `test.js` files or other packages.\n\nOne limitation: a quoted field that spans more than one line still won't parse, because rows are split on newlines before the parser runs. Nothing in the current data does this."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1036,"cache_read_input_tokens":32058,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1036},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7763afc6-6cbb-4c68-bdd2-4491627cfc8c","uuid":"feb6146f-42d6-4606-a676-afb0e62caa3d","timestamp":"2026-10-14T23:28:45.148Z","request_id":"req_011Cg2zpNsgFe128N7zrW773"}
